  "### Scaled Ingredients\n- Eggplant: 20 large (approx. 25 lbs)\n- Zucchini: 25 medium (approx. 15 lbs)\n- Yellow Squash: 25 medium (approx. 15 lbs)\n- Roma Tomatoes: 60 large (approx. 20 lbs)\n- Red Bell Peppers: 20 large\n- Yellow Onions: 15 large\n- Garlic: 10 heads (minced)\n- Crushed Tomatoes (Canned): 3 x #10 cans\n- Olive Oil: 1.5 liters\n- Fresh Thyme: 4 bunches\n- Fresh Basil: 4 bunches\n- Kosher Salt: 8 ounces (to taste)\n- Black Pepper: 3 ounces (to taste)\n\n### Revised Technique\nTo prevent a \"mushy\" consistency common in large-scale ratatouille, transition from the traditional stewing method to a **\"Deconstructed Roast & Fold\"** technique. Roast the eggplant, zucchini, and squash on oiled sheet pans in a convection oven at 425°F (220°C) until caramelized and slightly firm. This removes excess water and sets the exterior texture. Separately, build a thick, concentrated tomato and onion base (Piperade) in a tilt skillet. Gently fold the roasted vegetables into the base just before service or during the final 10 minutes of heating. This ensures each vegetable retains its distinct shape and bite despite the 75-lb total weight.\n\n### Workflow & Station Setup\n1. **Station Setup (Prep):** Establish a three-person prep line. Station 1: Peeling and dicing onions/garlic. Station 2: Dicing eggplant (larger cubes to account for shrinkage). Station 3: Dicing zucchini, squash, and peppers (uniform 1-inch cubes).\n2. **Staging:** Organize all diced vegetables into perforated hotel pans to allow excess moisture to drain before cooking.\n3. **The Sear (High Heat):** Utilize a tilt skillet or multiple commercial-grade sheet pans for oven-roasting. Avoid traditional sautéing in a pot; the volume will cause steaming rather than browning.\n4. **The Base (Piperade):** In a large rondo, sweat onions and peppers until translucent, add garlic, then incorporate crushed tomatoes and thyme. Simmer to reduce by 15%.\n5. **Integration:** Fold roasted vegetables into the sauce base carefully to maintain structural integrity.\n6. **Cooling/Holding:** Transfer to shallow 2-inch hotel pans for service or rapid chilling.\n\n### Timing & Service\n- **Prep Time:** 2.5 hours (3-person team).\n- **Roasting/Component Cooking:** 45 minutes (staggered batches).\n- **Assembly/Simmer:** 20 minutes.\n- **Service Strategy:** If serving buffet-style, hold at 145°F (63°C) in shallow pans. Do not stack pans deeper than 2 inches to avoid crushing the bottom layer. If cooking ahead, blast chill and reheat in small batches (10-15 portions) to maintain vibrant colors and herb aromas.\n\n### Cost Estimate\n$285.50\n\n### Pitfalls & Mitigations\n- PITFALL: Excessive moisture release from eggplant making the dish watery. MITIGATION: Salt the diced eggplant 30 minutes prior to roasting and pat dry to remove 'bitter' water.\n- PITFALL: Mashing of vegetables during large-scale stirring. MITIGATION: Use a wide rubber spatula or clean gloved hands to gently fold components in a shallow rondo instead of using a heavy metal paddle or spoon.\n- PITFALL: Loss of fresh herb flavor due to extended holding times. MITIGATION: Fold in 50% of the basil and thyme during the simmer, and save the remaining 50% to be added as a fresh garnish immediately before service.\n- PITFALL: Over-salting due to reduction. MITIGATION: Only season the vegetable components lightly before roasting; perform the final seasoning adjustment of the entire batch only after the sauce has reduced to its final consistency.",
